# Token-Saving Skill — UI Test Automation + Fixes

Goal: write tests, fix failing elements/locators/asserts — minimum tokens in, minimum tokens out.

## Core rule

**I only look at what I give you. Never read whole files, repo maps, imports, or related specs unless I link them explicitly. If context is short, answer short.**

## Scope of task — I will name it before asking

- `[AUTO]` write/update a test from a spec snippet
- `[FIX]` element locator unstable / not found
- `[FIX-ASSERT]` assertion failing
- `[DEBUG]` test fails but I don't know why yet

## Input format (I paste this)

```
[type:AUTO|FIX|FIX-ASSERT|DEBUG]
[file:NAME lines:X-Y]
[error/assert:<exact line>]
[locator:<current one if any>]
[ask: <one specific question>]
```

## Output contract (hard)

- Only the requested deliverable: changed code snippet OR root cause line. Never both unless `[ask]` asks.
- `[AUTO]`  → test code only. No explanation.
- `[FIX]`   → `ROOT CAUSE: <1 line>` then `CHANGED: <snippet>`.
- `[FIX-ASSERT]` → expected-vs-actual one line, then changed assertion.
- `[DEBUG]` → ONE hypothesis, ONE probe step. Stop.
- No refactors, no "while I'm here" cleanup, no doc comments, no alternative approaches, no "your code is good but".
- Max 10 lines code per answer unless I request more.
- Markdown essays banned. Bold headers only, then raw snippet.

## Locator strategy when `[type:FIX]` element unstable or not found

Determine first: not-found vs not-visible vs detached — fix differs.

- **Not found** → iframe / shadow-root / different tab / dynamic render → scope to nearest stable container. Show changed root line only.
- **Not visible** → wrong wait state or wrong expect. Use Playwright auto-wait; explicit `waitFor` only when race proven. No blind `sleep`.
- **Detached** → re-query locator fresh each use; never cache a stale element handle.

Locator specificity order (fewest fixes):
`getByRole/getByText/getByTestId` (by-most-specific, matches ONE node) → CSS → XPath.
Never bare broad selector (`a`, `div`, `[class]`).

## Token-waste causes I avoid as the prompter

1. **Retry loop** — asking "verify and check… what could be the fix" = Cursor re-reads whole context every round trip. Each retry = full snapshot = burn. Ask ONE precise thing per turn.
2. **Whole-file context** — Cursor injects all open tabs + full file + related files into every request. Keep ONLY the 3-5 relevant lines visible in the tab. Close the rest.
3. **Vague ask → wide guess** — the model reads more and answers long. Tight `[type]` + exact error kills both.

## My reminder to self

Before sending to Cursor: close unused tabs, keep only the failing block on screen, run the failing test first and paste its real error line, set `[type]`, ask exactly one thing.